    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p7-theory",
     "replacement": "A **rational inequality** has a variable in a denominator, such as $\\frac{1}{x}<3$. The denominator can be positive or negative, so multiplying both sides by it may require reversing the inequality symbol. Instead, multiply both sides by the **square** of the denominator, which is positive wherever the original expression is defined.\n\nTo solve a rational inequality:\n\n1. Multiply both sides by the square of the denominator, then solve the resulting quadratic or cubic inequality.\n2. Exclude any value that makes the original denominator zero.\n\nDo not cancel factors without keeping the original denominator restrictions: cancelling can change the domain, lose excluded critical points or admit invalid solutions.",
